{
 "fps": 30,
 "lead": 0.8,
 "tail": 2.0,
 "footer": "Sources: canada.ca \u00b7 CRA RRSP options at 71, RRIF minimums, T4127 (2026), OAS recovery tax, TFSA \u00b7 Examples, general info, not advice",
 "vo": [
  {
   "id": "L1",
   "file": "vo/L1.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Ten thousand dollars from the same R R S P. Taken out at sixty-two, the tax is about two thousand and fifty-five dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L2",
   "file": "vo/L2.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Taken out at seventy-two, it can cost four thousand, seven hundred and one."
  },
  {
   "id": "L3",
   "file": "vo/L3.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Same person. Same money. More than double the cost."
  },
  {
   "id": "L4",
   "file": "vo/L4.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The difference is a window that opens when you retire, and closes for good a few years later. Most people walk right past it."
  },
  {
   "id": "L5",
   "file": "vo/L5.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "By the end of this video, you'll know when your window is open, how much to take out, and the one mistake that wipes out the benefit."
  },
  {
   "id": "L6",
   "file": "vo/L6.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "All numbers: twenty twenty-six rates, Ontario, a single person. Examples, not real people. General information, not advice."
  },
  {
   "id": "L7",
   "file": "vo/L7.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "First: what the window is."
  },
  {
   "id": "L8",
   "file": "vo/L8.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Meet Ellen. She retired at sixty-one. She has R R S P savings and no workplace pension. She plans to start O A S later."
  },
  {
   "id": "L9",
   "file": "vo/L9.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So for a few years, Ellen has almost no taxable income. No salary. No pension yet. Only what she chooses to take out."
  },
  {
   "id": "L10",
   "file": "vo/L10.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That's the window. Low income, low tax brackets, and nobody forcing her to take anything."
  },
  {
   "id": "L11",
   "file": "vo/L11.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, why waiting can cost more."
  },
  {
   "id": "L12",
   "file": "vo/L12.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "An R R S P can't stay an R R S P forever. It has to be closed by the end of the year you turn seventy-one. Most people move it into a R R I F."
  },
  {
   "id": "L13",
   "file": "vo/L13.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And a R R I F comes with a rule: a minimum amount has to come out every year, set by your age."
  },
  {
   "id": "L14",
   "file": "vo/L14.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Now picture Ellen at seventy-two. Her R R I F minimum, plus her C P P, plus her O A S, all arrive in the same years."
  },
  {
   "id": "L15",
   "file": "vo/L15.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Say her net income is one hundred thousand dollars. What does the next ten thousand cost her?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L16",
   "file": "vo/L16.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Income tax: about three thousand, two hundred and one dollars. That's her federal and Ontario brackets."
  },
  {
   "id": "L17",
   "file": "vo/L17.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And because she's above ninety-five thousand, three hundred and twenty-three, the O A S clawback takes fifteen percent more. One thousand, five hundred dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L18",
   "file": "vo/L18.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Total: four thousand, seven hundred and one dollars. Almost half of that ten thousand."
  },
  {
   "id": "L19",
   "file": "vo/L19.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now the window."
  },
  {
   "id": "L20",
   "file": "vo/L20.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Ellen at sixty-two takes forty thousand dollars out of her R R S P. That's her whole income for the year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L21",
   "file": "vo/L21.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The tax on all forty thousand: about five thousand, one hundred and eleven dollars. Twelve point eight percent on average."
  },
  {
   "id": "L22",
   "file": "vo/L22.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And the next ten thousand at that level? About two thousand and fifty-five. Mostly the lowest federal and Ontario brackets."
  },
  {
   "id": "L23",
   "file": "vo/L23.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So the same ten thousand dollars costs two thousand and fifty-five in the window, and four thousand, seven hundred and one later. A difference of two thousand, six hundred and forty-six."
  },
  {
   "id": "L24",
   "file": "vo/L24.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "If Ellen does this for ten years, she moves four hundred thousand dollars out of her R R S P at those low rates, before the minimums and pensions stack up."
  },
  {
   "id": "L25",
   "file": "vo/L25.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "But here's the trap. If the window is good, why not take everything at once?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L26",
   "file": "vo/L26.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Because the brackets work per year. Say Ellen takes one hundred thousand dollars in a single year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L27",
   "file": "vo/L27.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The tax: about twenty-one thousand, five hundred and twenty-one."
  },
  {
   "id": "L28",
   "file": "vo/L28.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Spread over two years, fifty thousand each, the same hundred thousand costs about fourteen thousand, three hundred and thirty-two."
  },
  {
   "id": "L29",
   "file": "vo/L29.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Taking it in one shot costs more than seven thousand dollars extra. The window rewards steady amounts, not one big withdrawal."
  },
  {
   "id": "L30",
   "file": "vo/L30.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So how much each year? A simple target: fill the lowest brackets, and stop."
  },
  {
   "id": "L31",
   "file": "vo/L31.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "In Ontario, the first provincial bracket ends at fifty-three thousand, eight hundred and ninety-one. The first federal bracket ends at fifty-eight thousand, five hundred and twenty-three."
  },
  {
   "id": "L32",
   "file": "vo/L32.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Taking out up to about there keeps each dollar in the lowest rates. Above that, every extra dollar is taxed more."
  },
  {
   "id": "L33",
   "file": "vo/L33.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And the money doesn't have to be spent. If Ellen doesn't need it, she can move it into her T F S A, if she has the room."
  },
  {
   "id": "L34",
   "file": "vo/L34.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Inside the T F S A, it grows tax-free, and later withdrawals don't count as income, so they don't touch her O A S."
  },
  {
   "id": "L35",
   "file": "vo/L35.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "There's one more payoff. A smaller R R I F at seventy-two means smaller minimums, and a better chance of staying under the O A S line."
  },
  {
   "id": "L36",
   "file": "vo/L36.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And if Ellen had a spouse, from sixty-five, R R I F payments can be split on paper too. That spreads the income across two sets of brackets."
  },
  {
   "id": "L37",
   "file": "vo/L37.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Where this changes. These numbers leave out credits like the age amount and the pension income amount, so real bills can be a bit lower."
  },
  {
   "id": "L38",
   "file": "vo/L38.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "With a very low income, withdrawals can reduce income-tested benefits like the Guaranteed Income Supplement. That changes the math."
  },
  {
   "id": "L39",
   "file": "vo/L39.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And returns, health, and how long you'll need the money all matter. A tax professional can run your own numbers."
  },
  {
   "id": "L40",
   "file": "vo/L40.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Here's your checklist."
  },
  {
   "id": "L41",
   "file": "vo/L41.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "One. Find your window: the years between your last paycheque and your pensions and R R I F minimums."
  },
  {
   "id": "L42",
   "file": "vo/L42.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Two. Each year, take out enough to fill the low brackets. Not much more."
  },
  {
   "id": "L43",
   "file": "vo/L43.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Three. Don't take it all in one year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L44",
   "file": "vo/L44.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Four. Move what you don't need into your T F S A, if you have room."
  },
  {
   "id": "L45",
   "file": "vo/L45.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Five. Plan the window together with your C P P and O A S start dates."
  },
  {
   "id": "L46",
   "file": "vo/L46.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So, what did we learn?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L47",
   "file": "vo/L47.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "One. The same R R S P dollar can cost twice as much tax later."
  },
  {
   "id": "L48",
   "file": "vo/L48.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Two. The years right after you retire are the cheapest years to take it out."
  },
  {
   "id": "L49",
   "file": "vo/L49.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Three. Steady amounts beat one big withdrawal."
  },
  {
   "id": "L50",
   "file": "vo/L50.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Four. The T F S A keeps the money growing without touching your O A S."
  },
  {
   "id": "L51",
   "file": "vo/L51.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Next on this channel: the Canada child benefit, and how the way you file can change what your family receives. Subscribe, so you don't miss it."
  }
 ],
